    "body": "## Overview\n\nRefs #64204. This addresses only the separate Windows source-encoding side note, not the torch 2.11 Triton kernel side-table failure.\n\nRead traced Python sources with `tokenize.open` in both compile-cache paths so their contents and hashes do not depend on the process locale.\n\n## Claims\n\n- AOT source verification and JIT cache hashing now use Python's source-encoding rules, including UTF-8 defaulting and PEP 263 encoding cookies.\n- A forced-cp1252 regression proves that UTF-8 source produces the same hash as its actual content instead of a hash of locale-decoded text.\n\n## Validation\n\nNew regression, run against the unmodified production files and then the fixed files:\n\n```bash\nVLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=cpu uv run --no-project .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/compile/test_aot_compile.py::test_compute_code_hash_uses_source_encoding -v\n```\n\n- Baseline: 1 failed. The computed hash differed from `_compute_code_hash_with_content` because cp1252 decoded the UTF-8 bytes into different text.\n- Fixed: 1 passed. The test was run again after restoring the fixed files and passed again.\n\nComplete affected test file on the same environment:\n\n```bash\nVLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=cpu uv run --no-project .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/compile/test_aot_compile.py -q\n```\n\n| Production sources | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Baseline plus the new regression | 10 failed, 20 passed, 2 skipped |\n| Fixed | 9 failed, 21 passed, 2 skipped |\n\nThe only failure-set difference is the new regression changing from failed to passed. The other 9 failures are identical on both runs and come from the existing local CPU/native-AOT environment. In particular, the existing real-LLM GPT2 cache test fails during engine initialization on both versions; it is not counted as a successful validation.\n\nPre-commit on all three changed files passed, including ruff check, ruff format, mypy, typos, and the Buildkite test-tethering check:\n\n```bash\nuv run --no-project --with pre-commit pre-commit run --files vllm/compilation/caching.py vllm/compilation/backends.py tests/compile/test_aot_compile.py\n```\n\nNot run: a Windows server, CUDA execution, a real model AOT roundtrip, or model evaluation. This change affects source decoding and cache identity, not model outputs. No performance claim is made.\n\n## Details\n\nOn a non-UTF-8 Windows locale, an unqualified text `open` can decode UTF-8 source into different text or reject it. The AOT source check can then report a source mismatch and recompile, while the sibling JIT path can omit the unreadable file from its hash.\n\n`tokenize.open` is the standard Python source reader: it defaults to UTF-8 and honors an explicit PEP 263 coding cookie. Applying it to both existing readers fixes the root cause without introducing another hash implementation or changing inference behavior. Correcting a previously locale-dependent hash can cause a one-time cache miss after upgrade, which is safer than deriving cache identity from incorrectly decoded text.\n\nThis is not a duplicate of #62628. That PR consolidates the JIT/AOT hashing implementations and tolerates unreadable files; it does not fix their source decoding. The changes are complementary. If #62628 lands first, the same decoding change is needed in its consolidated helper.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\nAI assistance (Claude Code) was used for implementation and drafting. I reviewed every changed line and ran the validation above.\n",
